'''''Oloptum miliaceum'''''<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hamasha |first1=Hassan R. |last2=von Hagen |first2=K. Bernhard |last3=Röser |first3=Martin |date=2012-02-01 |title=Stipa (Poaceae) and allies in the Old World: molecular phylogenetics realigns genus circumscription and gives evidence on the origin of American and Australian lineages |url=http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00606-011-0549-5 |journal=Plant Systematics and Evolution |language=en |volume=298 |issue=2 |pages=351–367 |doi=10.1007/s00606-011-0549-5 |bibcode=2012PSyEv.298..351H |s2cid=254057542 |issn=0378-2697|url-access=subscription }}</ref> is a species of [[Poaceae|grass]] known by the common name '''smilograss'''.<ref>{{PLANTS|id=PIMI3|taxon=Piptatherum miliaceum|accessdate=6 October 2015}}</ref> It is native to Eurasia but it can be found in many other parts of the world as an [[introduced species]] and a casual [[weed]] of disturbed areas. It is a clumping perennial grass producing sturdy, erect stems that can reach 1.5 meters tall. The [[inflorescence]] is a [[panicle]] of several whorls of branches that divide into secondary branches bearing clusters of spikelets.

